  "text_md": "To protect ; to accompany. Yorksh. A young plant a shoot.\n\nSET-OPE. or door is set or kept open.\n\nA young plant a shoot.\n\nSet hare's head to the tit for tat. the\n\nSETS. The plaits of ruffs.\n\ngambrel. Yorksh.\n\nSET-SPEECH. speech   carefully   prepared and studied before it is delivered ỉn public.\n\n- (11 To settle ; to bind. Var. dial\n- (12 To place to account. (A-s)\n\n\nSETTE. Ruled. Scott.\n\n(13) the Towneley Mysteries, pp. 97, 118, as He that \" sett alle on seven,\" i.e., set or appointed everything in seven days. A similar phrase at p. 85 is not so evident. It is explained in the glossary, \" to set things in, to put them in order,\" but it evidently impUes in some cases an exactly opposite meaning, to set in confusion, to rush to battle, as in the following examples. \" To set the Steven, to agree upon the time and place of meeting previous to some expedition,\" West, and Cumb. Dial. p. 390. These phrases may be connected with each other. Be this as it may, hence is certainly derived the phrase to be at sixes and sevens, to be in gi'cat confusion. Herod, in his anger at the Wise Men, says, Bot be thay past me by, by Mahowne in heven,\n\nSETTEN-ON. Short in growth. North .\n\nor cow, into which helleboraster, called setterwork, being put, an issue is made for ill-humours to vent themselves. North. An\n\n(2) An accuser. Coles.\n\nSÉTTER-GRASS. Theherb bear's-foot.   Yorksh. Spelt setyrgrise in Nominale MS.\n\nSETTER-OUT. An editor, or author.\n\nSETTING. The west, called because the quarter of the sun. setting\n\nSETTING-DOWN. Said of a hawk when put into the mew. Gent. Rec. ii. 63.\n\nSETTING-PIN. A dibble. Glouc. Debbyll Or settyng stycke, Huloet, 1552\n\nSETTING-STICK. stick the plaits or sets of ruffs. making\n\n(1) (2) A long seat, generally one with a long back to it. North. It is an archaism. See Setille. SETTLE-BED. A folding bed. SETTLE-STONES. Stones at the edge of a\n\nBot he thay past me by, by Mahowne In heven, shalle, and that in hy, set alle on Trow ye 8 kyng as wyll suffre thaym to neven to have mastry bot myself fulle even. Any\n\nTowneley Mysteriee, P. 143.\n\nSETTLE-STONES. Stones at the edge of gutter in a cow-house. North.\n\nThus he settez on 8evene with his sekyre knyghttez\n\nSEU. Suit. Hearne.\n\nThe duk swore by gret God of hevene. Wold my hers so evene,\n\nditch; a drain. North. \" The towne sinke, the common sew,\" Nomenclator, 1585, p. 391. SEUNE. Seven. Cumb.\n\nWold hors my\n\nSet wold sett all one geven\n\nffor Myldor the swet Degrevant, 1279. Nor carelesly set all at sir and seven.\n\nSCUNE: Cumb. Seven.\n\nSEUREMENT. Security, generally in the legal sense. used\n\nSETE. (A.s) city.\n\nSEURETEE. Certainty. (A-N)\n\nThere ys gyant of renowne gret\n\nSEVEN-NIGHT. A week. This word occurs in The French Alphabet, 1615, P. 18.\n\nHe dystrowythe bothe sete and towyn.\n\nTorrent of Portugal, P.39.\n\nHe levyth not oon sevenyghte_\n\n# SETEWALE. The herb valerian. Fykes, reisyn, dates,\n\nMS:\n\nFykes, relsyn, dates ,\n\nFf . ii. 38, f. 63. SEVEN-YEAR. \" Has a vile thief this seven year,\" Shakespeare. It was a proverbial expression for a long time. O, the body of a Gorge,\n\nAlmaund rys, pomme-garnates ,\n\nGy of Wartvike, p. 421.\n\n0, the body of Gorge,\n\nSETH. (1) Since. (A-s)\n\nwold had them heare\n\nNerer seth we wedyd ware,\n\nIn falch, wold chope them,\n\ncare ; Bot now we must per[t]e a-two, Do thou the best, fore I must go. MS. Ashmole 61,\n\nware not 80 hack thls seven veere Mariage 0f Witt and WVisdome, 1579. Thay\n\ncan then thanke Sensuall Apetyte\n\nMS.\n\n(2) A scythe. Nominale SETHE. To boil. {A.-S.)\n\nTo boil. (4.-s) SET-HEDGE.\n\nquickset hedge. East. SETILLE. Seat. (4-s)\n\nInterlude of the Four Elements, n. d. SEVERALS. Portions of common assigned for a term to a particular proprietor, the other commoners waiving for the time their right of common over them. See Hunter on Shakespeare, i. 267. SEVERY. A division or compartment of a\n\nFowles of heven er prowdc Inow that wald heghe thalre setille aboven alle other fesshe of the se.\n\nApon the setyl of hys majesté\n\nSEVERY. division or compartment of vaulted cciling. Severous of a howsc;' MS. Dictionary, 1540.\n\nThat day Bal alle men before hym be.\n\nHampole, MS. Bowes, P. 180. SCTLINGS. Saplings.\n\nplanted in the religion of Christ, and have taken no sure root in the same, are easily moved as young tellings. Becon's fVorkSfP. 10.\n\nLinc.\n\n(2) Sowed. (3) To wipe ))eak, a term in ancient hawking given by Berners.\n\n",
